 @KristaTorres

@jasmineandtea 

Craving sushi? Here’s a fun alternative to try. 

Start by cutting a slit in a seaweed sheet to divide it into quarters, then fill each with a different ingredient–I used rice and furikake, sliced salmon, spicy mayo, and lotus root salad. Fold the quarters over each other and enjoy as a seaweed wrap.

@BrittneeCiara 

Store-bought puff pastry sheets make it super easy to whip up a little nosh to pregame your Thanksgiving meal. Simply cut the puff pastry into squares, stuff them into a greased muffin tins, and fill with whatever you'd like, no recipe needed! Bake at 400°F (200°C) for 20 minutes and you’ll have the perfect appetizer to share with everyone with just 15 minutes of active work! 

What are you filling your cups with?

@theboldappetite

French onion soup, but make it poutine! Cheesy, saucy, and irresistable, this French Onion Poutine takes fries to the next level. Crispy air fryer fries are smothered in a French onion gravy, then topped with Gruyère and cheese curds – these might just be the best fries you’ll ever have. SAVE this recipe!

Full recipe with step-by-step instructions can be found on BoldAppetite.com.

@erica_eatseverything

@laceliachicago is one of my favorite spots in Chicago for many reasons. Not only do they offer a Latin fusion menu, it's family-owned and the artwork all around makes you feel like you're somewhere in Latin America. 

They have one of the most unique dishes I have ever had, which is their chilaquiles torta! There are so many interesting stories about how Latin dishes were invented and created here in the USA, just like the jibarito and the Cuban sandwich. 

Celebrating Latine Heritage Month never tasted so good. Who's had a chilaquiles torta before?

@jasmineandtea 

This is samgyetang, a Korean ginseng chicken soup that is traditionally eaten on the hottest days of summer. The idea is to “fight fire with fire” to replenish all of the nutrients lost in the heat. 

Here, I’m using a ready-made packet from the Korean grocery store for convenience, but all of these ingredients can also be bought separately.

@jasmineandtea 

You’ve probably heard of fermented kimchi that takes weeks to prepare, but this is called baechu geotjeori (배추겉절이), or fresh kimchi, and it’s ready in just about an hour! Fermented kimchi is usually quite sour, but fresh kimchi is more mild in taste. It’s also not meant to last a long time, so it should be eaten on the same day for the best taste.

Recipe on jasmineandtea.com [ search ‘kimchi’ ]

